{"text": "BRONX BOY SLAIN; ZIP GUN IS SOUGHT: Conference Earlier in the Day ...\nNew York Times (1923-Current file); Dec 20, 1953;\nProQuest Historical Newspapers: The New York Times (1851-2009)\n\npg. 28\n\nBRONX BOY SLAIN;\nZIP GUN 15 s\u00a2 SOUGHT\n\nConference Earlier in the Day\nDiscusses Ways to Combat\nUse of Homemade Pistol |\n\nA 15-year-old Bronx boy was!\nfatally wounded last night by\nwhat the police believe was a \u201czip\ngun.\u201d Oniy a few hours earlier zip\nSuns, which are homemade weap-\nons, had been the subject of a spe-\ncial conference on ways to combat\nthem.\nThe youth, Jerry Piro of 703\nEast 187th Street, died at Ford-\nham Hospital about four hours\nafter he was found at 8:23 P. M.\nlying in a hallway at 701 East\n180th Street, seven blocks from his\n|home, with a bullet wound in his\nskull.\nIn the basement of the building\nthe police found a clubroom ap-\n\n[parently belonging to a youth\n\u2018gang known as the Imperial\nHoods and containing at least six\nzip guns.\nThe Piro boy was believed to\nhave been the victim of a juvenile\ngang war, and at least fifteen\nvyouths were seized by the police\n'for questioning in the slaying.\nMethods of combating the \u201czip-\ngun evil\u201d were discussed yesterday\nafternoon by sportsmen and State,\nAssembiymen Thomas A. Dwyer\nand Frank J. Pino of Brooklyn.\nThe zip gun, or homemade pistol\nused by teen-aged delinquents, was\nthe subject of a conference at the,\nAstor Hotel of representatives of!\nthe National Rifle Association of\nAmerica, the Associated Sports-\nmen's Council on Firearms, the\nNew York State Conservation\nCouncil and court authorities.\nThey recommended a re-exami-\nnation and r\u00e9vision of present fire-\narms laws by a joint legislative\ncommittee and a program to edu-|\ncate and train youth in proper use\nof firearms.\nThe re-examination of the fire-\narms laws was proposed as part\nof an effort to devise regulations)\n\nthat would eliminate zip guns and\nindiscriminate use of .22-calibre\nammunition without at the same\ntime affecting more responsible\nusers of firearms.\nThose attending the conference\nwere Maj. Gen. Merritt A. Edson,\nexecutive director of the National\nRifle Association; Special Sessions\nJudge John E. Cone; Michael)\n;Nadel and Michael Petruska of the\nNew York State Conservation\nCouncil; Arthur Wagoner of the\nAssociated Sportsmen\u2019s Council on\nFirearms; Mr. Dwyer and Mr.\nYimo; and J. Kenneth McCabe, as-\nsistant district attorney of Kings:\nCounty.\n\nReproduced with permission of the copyright owner. Further reproduction prohibited without permission.",
